WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
Look at this rigid bridge structure.

2
00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:08.880
For years, the Legacy React native bridge required all native module communication to be serialized,

3
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.920
passed across an asynchronous chasm, and parsed on the other side.

4
00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:14.760
That structure is obsolete.

5
00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:18.560
It's been replaced by a unified, high-speed fluid stream.

6
00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.560
Instead of relying on runtime discovery and JSON serialization, the new architecture relies

7
00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:27.600
on a strictly verified, compile-time C++ pipeline.

8
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:32.320
We are going to break down the exact mechanics of this pipeline, strictly focusing on the four-layer

9
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.440
implementations specific to iOS systems.

10
00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:40.280
The objective is to map the entire JSI to Swift pipeline and the module resolution chain

11
00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:43.120
to eliminate legacy communication overhead.

12
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.920
By moving integration checks away from unpredictable runtime environments and forcing deterministic

13
00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.400
compile-time build failures, you guarantee the stability of your mobile infrastructure before

14
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.920
a single line of JavaScript executes.

15
00:00:54.920 --> 00:01:00.680
This structural diagram shows the four-layer architecture of an iOS turbo module.

16
00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:05.640
Notice the top layer, the TypeScript spec, initiating the process by sending a verified

17
00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.680
data packet downward.

18
00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:12.600
This spec acts as the absolute build-time contract for your native environment.

19
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.480
That contract passes to the second layer, CodeGen, which automatically translates the TypeScript

20
00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:22.160
spec into C++ native interfaces during the build process.

21
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.640
Next is the objective C++ file.

22
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:30.080
This glue code bridges the JSI C++ definitions to the native iOS system.

23
00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.800
Finally, we reach the Swift layer, where the actual business logic lives.

24
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:40.080
Because Swift lacks raw C++ interrupt compatible with JSI's calling convention, the objective

25
00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.440
C++ layer is mandatory to connect the two.

26
00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.760
To make this connection, the string pass to the RCT export module macro in your native code

27
00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:54.360
must perfectly match the string used in turbo module registry.strictget on the JavaScript side.

28
00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:59.480
If there is even a minor casing mismatch across this boundary, JavaScript will fail to locate

29
00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:03.920
the module at runtime, but that is one of the rare runtime errors.

30
00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.560
If a method is declared in the spec but missing from the objective C++ implementation, the Xcode

31
00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:13.360
build will intentionally fail with a protocol conformance error.

32
00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:18.640
This four-layer strictness allows the framework to bypass JSON serialization completely.

33
00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.640
If the module compiles, the contract is intact.

34
00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:26.160
If the build complete, execution shifts to runtime, the exact moment JavaScript invokes

35
00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.880
turbomoduleregistry.get.

36
00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:34.560
JSI routes this look up to the RCT turbomodule manager, the central iOS object responsible for

37
00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:36.640
resolving module requests.

38
00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:41.360
This manager lives in the app delegate during app startup, or is managed internally by RCT

39
00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:45.040
host in React Native 0.74 as Bredgeless mode.

40
00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:48.200
This logic tree illustrates the manager consulting its delegate.

41
00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:53.120
An incoming JavaScript request hits the master node and filters down through three sequential

42
00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:54.640
priority gates.

43
00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.640
The first gate is GetModule class from name, which checks the registry to see if the requested

44
00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:01.440
native class exists.

45
00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.720
If the class exists, the resolution moves to the second gate, GetModule instance from class,

46
00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.960
which physically allocates the objective C or Swift object into memory.

47
00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:16.560
The third gate, GetModule, acts as the final fallback mechanism for pure C++ modules that

48
00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.640
lack an objective C class.

49
00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:23.760
If this final C++ fallback returns a null pointer, the system silently routes the request back

50
00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.120
to the legacy resolution path, or crashes.

51
00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.760
Mastering this specific delegate chain gives engineers absolute control over exactly how and

52
00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:35.480
when native objects are instantiated by the JavaScript runtime.

53
00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:39.400
By default, turbomodules remain in a state of lazy initialization.

54
00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:45.600
The object is not allocated into memory until the exact moment of the first JavaScript call,

55
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.960
keeping applications startup times low.

56
00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.440
You can override this behavior.

57
00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:55.520
Implementing requires main queue setup and returning yes, forces eager instantiation during

58
00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:00.540
app startup, which is necessary for modules that must configure global state before any

59
00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:01.880
code runs.

60
00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:07.560
For strict state isolation, adopting the RCT turbomodule shared nothing protocol forces

61
00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:12.760
the runtime to create per-color instances, rather than singletons, keeping data isolated

62
00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:15.280
across multiple rendering surfaces.

63
00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:19.960
Use these two parallel execution tracks on the left and asynchronous queue.

64
00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:24.720
On the right, a block of code merges directly into a C++ dispatch table node.

65
00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:29.920
For methods returning direct values rather than promises, execution is entirely synchronous.

66
00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.120
The ASIN queue is bypassed completely.

67
00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:37.200
JSI calls directly into the C++ dispatch table, allowing the return value to flow back to

68
00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.760
JavaScript in the exact same stack frame.

69
00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:45.440
Combining precise memory allocation control with synchronous single stack frame execution

70
00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:50.680
raises the performance ceiling of react native applications to near native speeds.

71
00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:55.440
The standard code gen protocol has limitations when building highly specialized infrastructure

72
00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.600
tools, like custom performance tracers or crash reporters.

73
00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:06.400
To bypass these limitations, modules can adopt the RCT turbomodule with JSI bindings protocol.

74
00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:11.120
This exposes a core function called install JSI bindings with runtime.

75
00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:16.000
This schematic shows how that function works, a data object bypasses the four layer code

76
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:22.400
gen stack entirely, and injects itself directly into the isolated JavaScript runtime container.

77
00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:26.000
Engineers can use this to install custom JSI host objects.

78
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.960
This custom injection occurs immediately after JSI initialization, but before the JavaScript

79
00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:32.800
bundle executes.

80
00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:38.040
Using direct JSI bindings, grants infrastructure engineers root access to the JavaScript

81
00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:43.520
execution environment, enabling deep system diagnostics that were impossible under the legacy

82
00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:44.520
bridge.

83
00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:49.800
The enforcement of strict compile time boundaries isolates native code, dramatically simplifying

84
00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:50.800
testing.

85
00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:56.200
Swift business logic can be unit-tested via xe-test, completely independent of JSI or the

86
00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:58.480
react native runtime setup.

87
00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:03.280
On the JavaScript side, mocking the TypeScript spec module allows testing via just without

88
00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:05.520
triggering native build requirements.

89
00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:10.040
For enterprise code bases heavily reliant on legacy native modules, an immediate rewrite

90
00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:11.360
isn't required.

91
00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:16.800
The react native 0.76 turbo module interrupt layer automatically wraps legacy modules in a

92
00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:18.920
turbo module facade.

93
00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:23.800
Engineers can maintain legacy RCT export method declarations alongside the new turbo module

94
00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:27.800
conformance, migrating method by method in a side-by-side transition.

95
00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:31.400
Cautionary, the interrupt layer does have caveats.

96
00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:37.440
Modules utilizing custom serial cues or legacy callback methods may behave unpredictably

97
00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:40.840
until they are fully migrated to the typed spec.

98
00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:44.720
Turbo modules on iOS enforce strict engineering boundaries.

99
00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:50.200
They move runtime chaos to compile time safety, eliminate asynchronous bottlenecks, and grant

100
00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:53.040
developers absolute control over native execution.

